#: src/macros/macro_rules/welcome.md:6
msgid "Macros By Example"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/welcome.md:8
msgid ""
"**Macros by Example** (also known as **Declarative Macros** or `macro_rules!"
"` macros) are the most common form of macros in Rust and the easiest to "
"write."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/welcome.md:11
msgid "They are defined using the `macro_rules!` construct."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/welcome.md:12
msgid ""
"They allow you to write custom pattern matches on tokens, expanding them "
"into standard Rust code."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/welcome.md:14
msgid ""
"They are partially hygienic: local variables and labels defined inside them "
"do not leak outside."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/welcome.md:16
msgid ""
"They introduce minimal compile-time overhead and have no external crate "
"dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/welcome.md:19
msgid ""
"We will learn how to write, configure, and match patterns in declarative "
"macros."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/welcome.md:24
msgid ""
"Mention that declarative macros are sometimes called \"macros by example\" "
"because you write an example of the syntax you want to match, and an example "
"of the output you want to produce. This is much like C preprocessor macros, "
"but with richer pattern matching."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/welcome.md:28
msgid ""
"Highlight that they are defined directly in your standard code, making them "
"convenient to add without extra setup."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/macro_rules/welcome.md:30
msgid ""
"This slide acts as a transition into how declarative macros are defined and "
"used."
msgstr ""
